    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Do solar cameras work in winter? How the season affects them</h2>
<p>So, do solar cameras work in winter? They do, but a solar camera is an energy budget. The panel adds energy during the day and the camera spends it around the clock. In summer, most setups run a large surplus. In winter, four things squeeze the budget at the same time:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Shorter days.</strong> Fewer daylight hours means fewer charging hours.</li>
<li><strong>Lower sun.</strong> Low winter sun is weaker, passes through more atmosphere and is more easily blocked by trees and buildings.</li>
<li><strong>Snow, frost and grime.</strong> Even a thin layer on the panel can stop charging.</li>
<li><strong>Cold batteries.</strong> Lithium-ion batteries hold less usable energy in the cold, and most cameras block charging below freezing to protect them.</li>
</ul>
<p>Meanwhile, the camera may use more energy than in summer: longer nights mean more hours of infrared night vision, and early darkness means more motion events recorded in the dark. Our explainer on <a href="/how-solar-cameras-work/">how solar cameras work</a> shows where the energy goes.</p>

<div class="keyfacts">
<div><b>~1–3 h</b><span>typical winter peak sun, northern areas</span></div>
<div><b>0 °C / 32 °F</b><span>common charging cutoff</span></div>
<div><b>−20 °C / −4 °F</b><span>typical lowest operating temp</span></div>
</div>

<h2>Solar security camera in winter: how much sun you actually get</h2>
<p>Peak sun hours (PSH) measure the daily solar energy at a site as hours of full, direct sun. A 5 W panel at 2 PSH with typical real-world losses of 20–40% might deliver roughly 6–8 Wh a day. That covers a moderately used camera at around 4 Wh a day, but a busy camera at around 8 Wh barely breaks even, and frosty mornings and snow cut the real harvest further. The table shows typical December–January ranges for a well-angled panel.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Region (typical)</th><th>Winter PSH</th><th>Summer PSH</th><th>Winter outlook for a small camera panel</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>US Southwest (AZ, NM, southern CA inland)</td><td>~4–5</td><td>~6–7</td><td>Comfortable for most cameras</td></tr>
<tr><td>US Southeast and Texas</td><td>~3–4</td><td>~5–6</td><td>Usually fine with sensible settings</td></tr>
<tr><td>US Mid-Atlantic and central states</td><td>~2–3</td><td>~5–6</td><td>Tight for busy cameras</td></tr>
<tr><td>US Northeast, Great Lakes, Upper Midwest</td><td>~1.5–2.5</td><td>~4.5–5.5</td><td>Often a deficit without changes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pacific Northwest</td><td>~1–1.5</td><td>~5–6</td><td>Deficit likely; larger panel advised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Southern Canada</td><td>~1.5–2.5</td><td>~5–6</td><td>Deficit likely for busy cameras</td></tr>
<tr><td>UK and Ireland</td><td>~0.5–1.5</td><td>~4–5</td><td>Hardest case; plan for top-ups</td></tr>
<tr><td>Southern Australia (June–Aug)</td><td>~2.5–3.5</td><td>~6–7</td><td>Usually fine with a good tilt</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<p>These are rough typical ranges; cloudiness, elevation and local terrain change them. For monthly figures at your exact address, use <a href="https://pvwatts.nrel.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NREL’s PVWatts calculator</a>, then plug your numbers into our <a href="/tools/solar-camera-battery-calculator/">solar camera battery calculator</a> to see whether your panel covers your camera’s daily use.</p>

<h2>Low winter sun means a steeper panel</h2>
<p>At 40° N, the midday sun in late December is only about 26–27° above the horizon. A panel tilted at a shallow summer angle catches that light at a glancing angle. Tilting the panel to roughly <strong>latitude + 15°</strong> faces it more squarely at the winter sun. At 40° N, that means about 55°.</p>
<p>A steep panel has two more winter benefits: snow slides off sooner and the low sun warms the dark surface, which helps it shed frost. Our full guide to the <a href="/guides/solar-camera-panel-angle/">best angle for a solar security camera panel</a> has a city-by-city tilt table, or use the <a href="/tools/solar-panel-angle-calculator/">solar panel angle calculator</a>.</p>
<p>Shade also changes in winter. Because the sun is low, a house, fence or evergreen that never touched the panel in summer can shade it for hours. Recheck the spot in November, not June. Our guide on <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-direct-sunlight/">whether solar cameras need direct sunlight</a> explains why partial shade hurts so much.</p>

<h2>Solar camera snow: what to do when the panel is covered</h2>
<p>A panel covered in snow produces close to nothing. Light, powdery snow often blows or slides off a steep panel within a day. Wet, heavy snow or ice can sit for days.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tilt steeply.</strong> At 50–60° or more, most snow slides off once the sun hits it.</li>
<li><strong>Clear it gently.</strong> Use a soft brush or a foam roof rake. Do not scrape with metal or chip at ice, which can scratch or crack the panel surface.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid hot water.</strong> Pouring hot water on a frozen panel risks thermal shock and leaves water to refreeze in the connector.</li>
<li><strong>Mount where you can reach.</strong> If you get regular snow, a panel you can brush from the ground or a step stool is far more useful than one high on the roof.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it clear of roof slides.</strong> Avoid mounting directly under a roof edge where sliding snow and ice can hit the panel.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Solar camera battery in cold weather</h2>
<p>Most solar cameras use lithium-ion cells. Cold affects them in two separate ways, and both are normal behavior rather than a fault.</p>

<h3>Why your camera shows “not charging” on frosty mornings</h3>
<p>Charging a lithium-ion battery below freezing can cause lithium plating, which permanently damages the cell and can create a safety risk. To prevent it, many cameras block charging when the battery is below about <strong>0 °C (32 °F)</strong>. On a cold, clear morning, the panel may be in full sun while the app reports “not charging” or a temperature warning. Once the battery warms above the threshold, often by late morning, charging resumes. If it never resumes even on milder days, see our <a href="/troubleshooting/not-charging/">solar camera not charging</a> troubleshooting guide.</p>

<h3>Capacity loss in the cold</h3>
<p>A cold lithium battery cannot deliver its full capacity. Exact figures depend on the cell chemistry, but losses of roughly 20–30% or more around −10 °C (14 °F) are a common typical range. The energy is not gone for good; it comes back when the battery warms. But on a run of freezing days, a camera with a nominally full battery can run out sooner than you expect.</p>

<h3>Operating temperature specs</h3>
<p>Many battery cameras list an operating range down to about <strong>−20 °C (−4 °F)</strong>, with some rated only to −10 °C (14 °F) and a few to −30 °C (−22 °F) or lower. Check the spec sheet before buying if you live where temperatures regularly drop below zero Fahrenheit. Cameras may shut down or behave erratically below their rated range and recover when it warms.</p>

<div class="callout info"><strong>Cold tip</strong><p>Mounting the camera under a small overhang, out of the wind, helps it hold a little more warmth and keeps snow off the lens. Keep the panel itself out in the open sun.</p></div>

<h2>Winter prep checklist for solar cameras</h2>
<p>Do this in mid-autumn, before the first hard frost.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Check winter shade</strong><p>Look at the panel at mid-morning, noon and mid-afternoon on a sunny day in late October or November. If shadows cross it, move it, raise it or use an extension cable.</p></li>
<li><strong>Steepen the tilt</strong><p>Set the panel to about latitude + 15°. Measure with a phone level app laid on the panel and tighten the bracket firmly.</p></li>
<li><strong>Clean the panel</strong><p>Wash off summer pollen, dust and bird droppings with water and a soft cloth.</p></li>
<li><strong>Inspect connectors and cable</strong><p>Make sure the plug is fully seated and any rubber cover is closed. Water that freezes in a loose connector can push it apart. Add a drip loop so water runs off before reaching the plug.</p></li>
<li><strong>Top up the battery</strong><p>Go into winter with the battery near full. If it is low, charge it indoors with the supplied cable before the cold arrives.</p></li>
<li><strong>Update firmware</strong><p>Updates sometimes improve power management and temperature handling. Do it while the battery is well charged.</p></li>
<li><strong>Switch to winter settings</strong><p>Apply the power-saving changes below, then watch the battery trend for two weeks.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2>Other winter problems: frost on the lens, condensation and Wi-Fi</h2>
<p>Power is the main winter issue, but not the only one. A few other cold-weather problems show up as blurry footage or missed clips and are easy to mistake for a charging fault.</p>
<h3>Frost, ice and snow on the lens</h3>
<p>Overnight frost or freezing fog can coat the lens and the PIR sensor window. The image turns milky and motion detection may become unreliable until the sun clears it. A small hood or mounting the camera under an eave reduces this a lot. Avoid wiping a frozen lens with anything abrasive; let it thaw or use a soft microfiber cloth.</p>
<h3>Condensation inside the housing</h3>
<p>Big swings between mild, damp days and freezing nights can cause condensation behind the lens on some cameras. Keep rubber port covers and the battery compartment seal closed tightly, and only open the camera indoors in dry conditions. If fogging persists inside a sealed camera, contact the maker, as it can indicate a failed seal.</p>
<h3>PIR detection in the cold</h3>
<p>Passive infrared sensors detect the heat difference between a moving body and its background. In winter, the contrast between a warm person and cold surroundings is large, so detection tends to be strong and sometimes too sensitive. Heavy coats reduce the contrast a little at long range. If you get bursts of false alerts from wind-blown snow or exhaust plumes, tighten the motion zone rather than turning sensitivity right down.</p>
<h3>Wi-Fi range and cold</h3>
<p>Cold does not weaken Wi-Fi, but a weak signal makes the camera spend longer transmitting each clip, which costs energy. Snow-loaded trees and wet surfaces can absorb some signal too. If your camera sits at the edge of Wi-Fi range, a mesh node or extender closer to it can noticeably reduce winter power use. Our guide on <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-wifi/">whether solar cameras need Wi-Fi</a> covers the alternatives, including cellular models.</p>

<h2>Settings that reduce winter consumption</h2>
<p>Settings often make a bigger difference than the panel. The goal is fewer, shorter recordings and less radio time.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Winter change</th><th>Why it helps</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Motion zones</td><td>Exclude roads, trees and bushes</td><td>Fewer false wake-ups from traffic and moving branches</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sensitivity</td><td>Lower one or two steps</td><td>Fewer triggers; PIR sensors can be jumpy in the cold because people stand out sharply against cold backgrounds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Smart detection</td><td>Person or vehicle only, if offered</td><td>Ignores animals and snowfall</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clip length</td><td>Shorten (for example 10–15 s)</td><td>Less recording and upload time per event</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retrigger interval</td><td>Lengthen</td><td>Stops back-to-back clips of the same event</td></tr>
<tr><td>Night vision</td><td>Use IR instead of spotlight or color night mode</td><td>Spotlights draw much more power during long nights</td></tr>
<tr><td>Live view</td><td>Check less often, shorter sessions</td><td>Live streaming is one of the biggest drains</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resolution</td><td>Drop one step if image quality allows</td><td>Smaller files, shorter uploads</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>To understand how many days your battery can carry the camera through a gray spell with these settings, read <a href="/guides/solar-camera-battery-life-no-sun/">how long solar cameras last without sun</a>. For night settings specifically, see <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-at-night/">do solar cameras work at night</a>.</p>

<h2>When to add a second solar panel</h2>
<p>If you have asked yourself “do solar cameras work in winter where I live?” and the battery trend says no, more panel is the answer. Watch the battery percentage in the app over a couple of weeks of typical winter weather. A healthy setup dips overnight and recovers on sunny days. If the level trends steadily downward even after you have fixed shade, tilt and settings, the panel is undersized for your winter.</p>
<p>Signs you need more panel:</p>
<ul>
<li>Battery falls 5–10% or more per week through December despite clear days.</li>
<li>You live in the Pacific Northwest, the northern tier of the US, Canada or the UK and have a busy camera on a 3–5 W panel.</li>
<li>The best available spot only gets a few hours of direct winter sun.</li>
</ul>
<p>Options depend on the camera. Some accept a larger panel or a second panel input; others support an external battery pack. Use the maker’s compatible accessories, since mismatched voltage or connectors can damage the camera and void the warranty. If you run several cameras, our guide on <a href="/guides/how-many-solar-cameras-can-one-panel-power/">how many solar cameras one panel can power</a> covers shared systems.</p>
<div class="callout tip"><strong>Plan for the worst month</strong><p>The real test of whether solar cameras work in winter is the darkest few weeks. A solar camera that copes in December will cope all year. Size the panel and pick the mounting spot for the darkest weeks, and the rest of the year takes care of itself.</p></div>
<p>If you are still choosing hardware, cameras with a separate panel, wide operating temperature range and good power settings handle winter best. See our <a href="/best-solar-security-cameras/">best solar security cameras</a> guide for what to look for.</p>
